Cell["\<\
Note: GainDB here is the power gain in dB; -999.99 is AntennaLink' s stand-in \
for \"essentially zero\" (a true null, like along the dipole axis) .\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.990200278402317*^9, 3.9902003013978643`*^9}},
 CellID->1281004633,ExpressionUUID->"5fa46c00-2057-47a0-b4da-d0fdf58040ae"],

Cell["\<\
Why? Each little piece of current radiates like a tiny dipole \[LongDash] \
strongest sideways, nothing off its ends. Add up all the pieces and the wire \
as a whole inherits that behavior: maximum broadside, null end-on. The \
current distribution shaped the pattern.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.990200072003278*^9},
 CellID->345249524,ExpressionUUID->"ac3ed989-9ce6-4203-a224-c52377464e46"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Directivity And Gain", "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.990200116549045*^9, 3.990200120844997*^9}},
 CellID->1862359520,ExpressionUUID->"a90f1e96-ec7f-4b2c-b36d-8a2ed6bf660c"],

Cell["\<\
A pattern that favors some directions must be weaker in others, because total \
radiated power is conserved. Directivity measures how much an antenna \
concentrates power compared to a hypothetical isotropic radiator (one that \
radiates equally in all directions). \
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.990200133133602*^9, 3.990200139978808*^9}},
 CellID->1601578572,ExpressionUUID->"022737b2-6db5-4027-a46a-b1df86c3e609"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["We quote it in dBi (decibels relative to isotropic):", "MathCaption",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.990200133133602*^9},
 CellID->1968643832,ExpressionUUID->"45b1bb5c-1d33-40d8-aa5d-0c51a557fe5e"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

