Cell[TextData[{
 "The bandwidth of an antenna is the frequency range over which the match is \
\"good enough\" \[LongDash] commonly where ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"VSWR", "<", "2"}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineMath",ExpressionUUID->
  "6182cf25-9914-49d3-a67e-89914f77fa62"],
 " (the line drawn above). Notice the lowest ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["VSWR", TraditionalForm]], "InlineMath",ExpressionUUID->
  "ad249c8d-ae83-4457-b8ea-70ceddea9e24"],
 " sits near resonance: matching and resonance are closely related, and much \
of practical antenna engineering is about moving these curves where you want \
them."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.990200487648039*^9, 3.990200499520952*^9}},
 CellID->790276355,ExpressionUUID->"6c733f2a-c3e9-4cb2-9d1b-44e82ec8d32c"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Polarization", "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.990200538528982*^9, 3.990200538660893*^9}, {
  3.990200573921234*^9, 3.990200577671625*^9}},
 CellID->2114846439,ExpressionUUID->"d90c9445-9d22-4cca-8381-b9cb7179bfe6"],

Cell["\<\
The wave from an antenna has its electric field pointing in a definite \
direction \[LongDash] that is its polarization. For a straight wire, the \
radiated E-field lines up with the wire. Our z-oriented dipole is therefore \
vertically polarized. A receiving antenna must share the transmitter's \
polarization, or it loses signal (a vertical and a horizontal dipole are, in \
theory, completely \"deaf\" to each other).\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.990200541943589*^9, {3.990200584043126*^9, 3.990200612546995*^9}},
 CellID->1157615777,ExpressionUUID->"e27da6a8-6bfb-4aa6-8b22-340279304f73"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
AntennaLink returns the field as two components, ETheta and EPhi (the two \
directions transverse to the direction of travel). Look broadside to the \
dipole:\
\>", "MathCaption",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.990200541943589*^9, 3.990200584043126*^9, 
  3.9902006145665197`*^9},
 CellID->1367136177,ExpressionUUID->"3e20ff12-cb7d-4be8-9c21-345d6f33bda8"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[{
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"b", " ", "=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"SelectFirst", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"Normal", "[", 
      RowBox[{"ff", "[", "\"\<FarField\>\"", "]"}], "]"}], ",", " ", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"#Theta", " ", "==", " ", "90.0"}], " ", "&&", " ", 
       RowBox[{"#Phi", " ", "==", " ", "0.0"}]}], " ", "&"}]}], "]"}]}], 
  ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"Abs", "[", 
    RowBox[{"b", "[", "\"\<ETheta\>\"", "]"}], "]"}], ",", " ", 
   RowBox[{"Abs", "[", 
    RowBox[{"b", "[", "\"\<EPhi\>\"", "]"}], "]"}]}], "}"}]}], "Input",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.990200541943589*^9, 3.990200584043126*^9, {3.9902006284206743`*^9, 
   3.9902006376135483`*^9}},
 CellLabel->"In[182]:=",
 CellID->1530187586,ExpressionUUID->"139026d3-e59a-44ac-b76e-df48cb1b62f7"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{"0.6627174264637996`", ",", "0.`"}], "}"}]], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.990200638033668*^9},
 CellLabel->"Out[183]=",
 CellID->1860885237,ExpressionUUID->"3f214c1f-17cc-4fd8-a2d5-4f9ee479ff6d"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
One component dominates and the other is ~0: that is linear polarization. \
When the two components are equal in size but 90\[Degree] out of phase, the \
field vector rotates as the wave travels \[LongDash] circular polarization, \
used for satellites and GPS.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.990200541943589*^9, 3.990200584043126*^9, {3.990200664527944*^9, 
   3.990200667597684*^9}},
 CellID->1746085077,ExpressionUUID->"923ef86e-53f2-4115-bc28-82d182987df4"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Making An Antenna Directional: The Yagi-Uda", "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.990200541943589*^9, 3.990200584043126*^9, {3.990200664527944*^9, 
   3.990200697991905*^9}},
 CellID->1841638568,ExpressionUUID->"6df5daa7-1fd9-49ce-abcb-66c14d91863b"],

Cell["\<\
A dipole spreads its power almost everywhere. To send it one way (more range, \
less interference) we add parasitic elements \[LongDash] extra wires that are \
not fed at all. They pick up the field from the driven dipole, re-radiate it, \
and if their lengths and spacings are right, the re-radiated waves add up in \
one direction and cancel in the other. This is the Yagi-Uda antenna \
\[LongDash] the classic \"TV aerial\".\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.990200541943589*^9, 3.990200584043126*^9, {3.990200664527944*^9, 
   3.990200670231249*^9}, 3.9902007193368483`*^9},
 CellID->1368711179,ExpressionUUID->"6dc431e3-816b-4e87-a96e-112f19dffac7"],

Cell["The recipe:    ", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.990200541943589*^9, 3.990200584043126*^9, {3.990200664527944*^9, 
